# once_nothing: S4 draws only that there "could have been" nothing, a possibility. S5 ("if this were true") and S6 ("if at one time nothing was in existence") need that nothing actually existed at some time. Only an actual time of nothing would leave nothing now.
# The reading's wording "could have been (was)" hides this difference. What makes the claim true differs: a mere possibility versus an actual past state.
# No premise or assumption carries the shift. C2 is drawn, not stated as a premise. P5 and C3 just use the actual sense, so I name no bridge.
# I was unsure about "necessary": necessary_exists and self_necessary seem to share one sense (cannot cease to be). Caused necessity versus necessity of itself is drawn inside that sense, not as a second sense.
